{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c601","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0b1f0-f342-7821-939e-4786eeeb7588"},"sequence":347,"recorded_at":1780531400000689,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"c7ad7d78-80b9-46e1-9367-f83a27084d43","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"c7ad7d78-80b9-46e1-9367-f83a27084d43","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"c7ad7d78-80b9-46e1-9367-f83a27084d43"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0b1f1-c312-7792-98f9-701e91ec5a2e"},"task_id":"01a0b1f1-c312-7792-98f9-701e91ec5a2e","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0b1f1-c312-7792-98f9-701e91ec5a2e","chunk":"{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"exec-14-1\",\n  \"command\": \"sed -n '9178,9330p' internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.7.0.md\",\n  \"description\": \"Read spec section 13.1 direct-session launch\",\n  \"exit_code\": 0,\n  \"terminal_status\": \"completed\",\n  \"output\": \"### 13.1 Direct-session launch\\n\\nPreconditions:\\n\\n- configuration and local host identity are valid;\\n- the session name is unused across the reachable mesh;\\n- the provider plugin is trusted and <code>probe</code> succeeds;\\n- requested profile mapping is available;\\n- destination workspace is validated or captured as a new managed workspace;\\n- the terminal backend is available; and\\n- no task-board fields are supplied.\\n\\nTransitions:\\n\\n1. Allocate session, caller-stable bootstrap-operation,\\n   first-checkpoint-operation, and initial lease IDs. If the workspace has a\\n   valid current managed-replica marker, reuse its unique Workspace Group\\n   Record after exact topology validation; otherwise allocate new workspace-\\n   group/workspace IDs and one new group record. Epoch is 1 and the local host\\n   is the holder.\\n2. Persist any new Workspace Group Record, then the Session Record referencing\\n   that group, the initial Lease Record, and <code>session.created</code> carrying\\n   both operation IDs before creating a process. Joining\\n   an existing group publishes no group update; derived membership changes by\\n   union of the new Session Record.\\n3. Create the durable terminal entry <code>ax pane SESSION_ID</code> with the\\n   bootstrap operation ID.\\n4. Call provider <code>launch</code> and validate its argv/env-name plan.\\n5. Start the provider through the terminal backend with the epoch-1 fencing\\n   token and persisted profile. Persist <code>provider.launched</code> with\\n   <code>execution_profile</code> equal to the Session Record and\\n   <code>profile_source_event_id = null</code>.\\n6. Call <code>identify-session</code>; persist the Provider Identity Record and\\n   <code>provider.identified</code> event.\\n7. When the first safe boundary is observed, create the workspace-group root\\n   manifest. If <code>portable_store</code> is enabled, call provider\\n   <code>native-store-plan phase=capture</code> and <code>capture</code> with the\\n   first-checkpoint operation ID into an\\n   isolated Object Sink; otherwise create a zero-entry provider manifest that\\n   references the exact Provider Identity Record. Validate both, then publish\\n   the direct-path checkpoint with a non-null provider manifest.\\n8. Publish immutable objects for replica sync and report <code>running</code>\\n   or <code>idle</code>.\\n\\nWhen the launch carries a caller-supplied plan (Section 14.1\\n<code>--launch-plan</code>), one step precedes step 2 for both document forms.\\n<code>ax</code> calls provider <code>launch</code> with the candidate Session\\nRecord in planning role — provider <code>launch</code> is already a\\nplan-returning operation that creates no process (Section 7.5) — takes the\\nreturned <code>SpawnPlan.argv</code> as the resolved final argv (the plugin's\\nbase argv followed by the document's <code>argv_suffix</code>; for the\\n<code>argv</code> form, the plugin's verbatim translation after its Section\\n7.7 profile-flag check), validates it under the Section 5.1 argv limits\\n(1..128 elements, each 1–4,096 bytes, at most 65,536 encoded bytes), and only\\nthen persists the record with <code>launch_plan.argv</code> equal to that\\nfinal argv, so the immutable record answers \\\"what launched\\\" without\\nre-deriving anything. Every refusal of this step — shape, limit, exclusivity,\\nunknown member, extension collision or bound, secret, profile flag, or\\nmissing capability — fires before any Session Record, lease, terminal entry,\\nor process exists. A plugin declaring <code>caller_launch_plan</code> MUST\\nanswer the planning call deterministically, and step 4's <code>launch</code>\\nagainst the persisted record MUST return an argv equal to the recorded one;\\na mismatch is <code>provider_protocol_error</code> and no process is created.\\n\\nThe record carries the caller document under the <code>ax</code>-generic\\ntop-level <code>extensions</code> key <code>ax.launch-plan-request</code>, a\\nclosed object\\n<code>{form: \\\"argv\\\" | \\\"argv_suffix\\\", base_argv_length: uint53, request_digest: digest}</code>.\\nThe <code>ax</code> label mirrors the <code>urn:ax</code> authority of every\\nschema identifier in Section 1.5. The document itself is not stored: the\\nSection 1.6 extension bound is 65,536 canonical bytes, and a document may\\ncarry 65,536 bytes of argv plus 65,536 bytes of stdin. For the same reason the\\nvalue carries no copy of the suffix: <code>launch_plan.argv</code> already\\nholds it, and the suffix is defined as\\n<code>launch_plan.argv[base_argv_length:]</code> (for the <code>argv</code>\\nform <code>base_argv_length</code> is 0 and the suffix is the whole argv).\\nThat definition is what resume replays (Section 7.5) and what lets a reader\\nsplit the recorded final argv into its two owners; the value is a few dozen\\nbytes regardless of the plan's size. <code>request_digest</code> is the\\nSection 1.6 SHA-256 digest of the JCS canonical bytes of the caller document\\nand lets an operator prove which document produced the record; it is a record,\\nnot a signature. The caller's own <code>extensions</code> — for a\\nCurator-composed document the four <code>works.relux.curator.*</code> keys of\\nSection 5.1 are among them; <code>ax</code> is generic and knows no such class\\n— together with this key MUST fit the Section 1.6 extensions bound (at most 64\\nkeys, canonical object at most 65,536 bytes) as they will be persisted; a\\ndocument that would not is refused in the planning step with\\n<code>launch_plan_invalid</code>, <code>field: \\\"extensions\\\"</code>, so no\\ndocument that passes validation can yield a record <code>ax</code> cannot\\npersist.\\n\\nThe caller-plan path has these required conformance cases; Appendix D binds\\nthem to <code>fixtures/launch_plan_request_conformance.json</code>:\\n\\n| Fixture | Input | Required result |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| <code>LAUNCH-PLAN-SUFFIX-POS</code> | <code>argv_suffix</code> document under <code>--profile standard</code> against a <code>caller_launch_plan</code> plugin | Record persisted with <code>launch_plan.argv</code> equal to the plugin's base argv followed by the suffix and <code>ax.launch-plan-request</code> <code>{form: \\\"argv_suffix\\\", base_argv_length: &lt;base length&gt;, request_digest}</code>; step 4 returns the recorded argv |\\n| <code>LAUNCH-PLAN-ARGV-POS</code> | <code>argv</code> document whose element 0 is the plugin-resolved executable, <code>--profile standard</code> | Plugin appends nothing; <code>base_argv_length</code> is 0; recorded argv equals the document's |\\n| <code>LAUNCH-PLAN-PROFILE-FLAG-NEG</code> | <code>argv</code> or <code>argv_suffix</code> carries the provider's own Section 7.7 <code>yolo</code> flag, long form or documented alias, under <code>--profile standard</code> | <code>launch_plan_invalid</code>, <code>reason: \\\"profile_flag\\\"</code>, <code>details.argv_index</code> the element's index in the final argv; no Session Record or process |\\n| <code>LAUNCH-PLAN-SECRET-NEG</code> | An <code>env_literals</code> value or <code>stdin</code> payload the Section 5.1 secret rule or Section 16.2 exclusions classify | <code>secret_policy_violation</code> (exit 16), <code>details.field</code> naming the member; no Session Record |\\n| <code>LAUNCH-PLAN-EXTENSIONS-NEG</code> | Caller <code>extensions</code> that with <code>ax.launch-plan-request</code> and the Curator keys exceed the Section 1.6 bound | <code>launch_plan_invalid</code>, <code>field: \\\"extensions\\\"</code>; no Session Record |\\n| <code>LAUNCH-PLAN-CAPABILITY-NEG</code> | Any document against a plugin whose manifest does not declare <code>caller_launch_plan</code> | <code>capability_unavailable</code>, <code>details.capability: \\\"caller_launch_plan\\\"</code>, before the plugin is invoked |\\n| <code>LAUNCH-PLAN-DETERMINISM-NEG</code> | Plugin returns at step 4 an argv different from its planning-role answer | <code>provider_protocol_error</code>; no process |\\n\\nEach negative case is proven by a test that fails when the gate admits the\\ninput, driven through the real <code>ax start --launch-plan</code> entry point;\\na delete-only mutant is insufficient (Appendix D.1), and the profile-flag case\\nMUST also survive a narrowing mutant that drops one documented alias.\\n\\nThe first-checkpoint operation ID keys the capture/export and checkpoint-\\npublication transaction even when a backend-only provider emits a zero-entry\\nmanifest. An identical retry reuses the same immutable objects and event;\\nchanging either operation ID after <code>session.created</code> is\\n<code>idempotency_mismatch</code>.\\n\\nUntil step 7 publishes the first Checkpoint, this is the closed\\n<em>bootstrap window</em>. The epoch-1 lease is authoritative with\\n<code>checkpoint_id = null</code>; the derived session state is\\n<code>creating</code> while a retry is eligible and <code>failed</code> after an\\nambiguous/live-process error or explicit abort. No implementation may invent a\\ncheckpoint ID or call that state <code>stopped</code>.\\n\\nFailure handling is phase-exact:\\n\\n| Fixture/boundary | Durable facts | Recovery |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| <code>BOOT-DIRECT-BEFORE-TERMINAL</code> | Session/lease exist; no terminal, process, identity, or checkpoint | <code>ax resume</code> may retry terminal creation under the same IDs after probe; force stop emits bootstrap abort |\\n| <code>BOOT-DIRECT-AFTER-TERMINAL</code> | Wrapper exists; no provider process or checkpoint | Resume reuses that wrapper and retries launch once; force stop removes only the inert wrapper after closure proof |\\n| <code>BOOT-DIRECT-AFTER-PROCESS</code> | Provider may be live; no identified identity/checkpoint | Resume MUST first reconcile exact PID/PTY and bootstrap operation; if liveness/identity is ambiguous, remain failed and require stop/reconciliation rather than start a second process |\\n| <code>BOOT-DIRECT-AFTER-IDENTITY</code> | Identity/event exist; no checkpoint | Resume validates the same identity and continues capture; it MUST NOT call provider new-session launch |\\n| <code>BOOT-DIRECT-BEFORE-CHECKPOINT</code> | Captured objects may exist but no Checkpoint Record is authoritative | Validate/reuse immutable objects and publish the first checkpoint, or abort; objects are never relabeled as a checkpoint |\\n\\nA bootstrap retry is allowed only at epoch 1, with a null lease checkpoint,\\nno authoritative <code>session.bootstrap_aborted</code>, and either proven no\\nprovider process or one exact reconciled process/identity from this launch.\\nThe retry reuses every already persisted operation/terminal/provider identity\\nand is idempotent; it never creates a second raw provider process. A graceful\\nstop may first reach a safe boundary and publish the initial checkpoint, then\\nexecute the ordinary checkpointed stop. A force stop may close the process and\\nemit <code>session.bootstrap_aborted</code> followed by\\n<code>session.stopped</code> with null checkpoint,\\n<code>resumable=false</code>, and <code>closure_kind=bootstrap_abort</code>; the\\nresulting lifecycle state is <code>failed</code>, not <code>stopped</code>.\\nClosure with an unknown/live process is unsuccessful and emits neither event.\\n\\n<code>BOOT-ABORT-CROSS-CONTRACT</code> is the exact successful force-closure\\nprojection for a failure after process creation and before identity:\\n\\n| Surface | Exact authoritative values |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| <code>session.bootstrap_aborted</code> payload | <code>{operation_id:BOOTSTRAP_ID,failure_phase:after_process,provider_identity_record_id:null,manager_session_ref:null,process_closed:true,store_closed:true,resume_allowed:false}</code> |\\n| Following <code>session.stopped</code> payload | <code>{graceful:false,checkpoint_id:null,resumable:false,closure_kind:bootstrap_abort,process_closed:true,store_closed:true}</code> |\\n| RPC <code>session.stop</code> success | <code>{operation_id:STOP_ID,already_applied:false,closure:{process_closed:true,store_closed:true,exit_code:EXIT_OR_NULL,remaining_process_handles:[],final_store_generation:null},final_checkpoint_id:null,resumable:false,bootstrap_aborted:true,resulting_state:failed}</code> |\\n\",\n  \"original_output_bytes\": 11938,\n  \"original_output_tokens\": 2985,\n  \"truncated\": false\n}","final_result":true}}}
